  <content>
    <section>
      <para>In this unit, remember to double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge, if needed.</para>
      <title>Dividing Polynomial Functions</title>
      <para>Just as polynomials can be divided, polynomial functions can also be divided.</para>
      <div>
        <para>
          <emphasis> DIVISION OF POLYNOMIAL FUNCTIONS </emphasis>
        </para>
        <hr/>
        <para>For functions \(f(x)\) and \(g(x)\), where \(g(x) \neq 0\),</para>
        <para>\((\frac{f}{g})(x)=\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\)</para>
      </div>
      <para>Example 1</para>
      <para>For functions \(f(x)=x^2-5x-14\) and \(g(x)=x+2\):</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
        <item>Find \((\frac{f}{g})(x)\).</item>
      </list>
      <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> \((\frac{f}{g})(x)=\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\)  Substitute for \(f(x)\) and \(g(x)\).</para>
      <para> \((\frac{f}{g})(x)=\frac{f(x)=x^2-5x-14}{x+2}\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Divide the polynomials using long division.</para>
      <para> \[\begin{array}{l}\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,x - 7\\x + 2\left){\vphantom{1{{x^2} - 5x - 14}}}\right.
  \!\!\!\!\overline{\,\,\,\vphantom 1{{{x^2} - 5x - 14}}}\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - ({x^2} + 2x)} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, - 7x - 14\\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\underline { - ( - 7x - 14)} \\\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,0\end{array}\]
  \((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x-7\)</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
        <item>Find \((\frac{f}{g})(-4)\).</item>
      </list>
      <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> In part (1), we found \((\frac{f}{g})(x)\).</para>
      <para> \((\frac{f}{g})(x)=x−7\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>To find \((\frac{f}{g})(-4)\), substitute \(x=-4\).</para>
      <para> \((\frac{f}{g})(-4)=-4-7\)
  \((\frac{f}{g})(-4)=-11\)</para>
      <title>Try It: Dividing Polynomial Functions</title>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>For functions \(f(x)=x^2-5x-36\) and \(g(x)=x+4\):</para>
            <para>Find \((\frac{f}{g})(x)\). </para>
            <para>Find \((\frac{f}{g})(-5)\). </para>
            <para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Here is how to solve these polynomial function division problems:</para>
            <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
              <item>\((\frac{f}{g})(x) = \frac{x^2-5x-36}{x+4}=x-9\) </item>
            </list>
            <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
              <item>\((\frac{f}{g})(-5)=x-9=(-5)-9=-14\) </item>
            </list>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <title>Using the Remainder Theorem</title>
      <para>Let’s look at some division problems and their remainders. They are summarized in the chart below. If we take the dividend from each division problem and use it to define a function, we get the functions shown in the chart. When the divisor is written as \(x - c\), the value of the function at \(c\), \(f(c)\), is the same as the remainder from the division problem.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-wideadjustedtable">
        <thead>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>Dividend</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>Divisor</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>Remainder</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>Function</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>\(f(c)\)</emphasis>
            </entry>
          </row>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(x^4-7x^2+7x+6\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>\(x - (-3)\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>\(3\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>\(f(x)=x^4-7x^2+7x+6\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>\(f(-3)=3\)</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(f(x)=3x^3-2x^2-10x+8\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>\(x - (-3)\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>\(-61\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>\(f(x)=3x^3-2x^2-10x+8\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>\(f(-3)=-61\)</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(x^4-16x^2+3x+15\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>\(x - (-4)\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>\(3\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>\(f(x)=x^4-16x^2+3x+15\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>\(f(-4)=3\)</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <para>To see this more generally, we realize we can check a division problem by multiplying the quotient times the divisor and adding the remainder. In function notation, we could say: To get the dividend \(f(x)\), we multiply the quotient, \(q(x)\), times the divisor, \(x-c\), and add the remainder, \(r\).</para>
      <para>\(f(x)=q(x)(x-c)+r\)</para>
      <para> If we evaluate this at \(c\), we get:
  \(f({\style{color:red}c})=q({\style{color:red}c})({\style{color:red}c}-c)+r\)
  \(f(c)=q(c)(0)+r\)
  \(f(c)=r\)</para>
      <para>This leads us to the Remainder Theorem.</para>
      <note>
        <para>
          <emphasis>REMAINDER THEOREM</emphasis>
        </para>
        <hr/>
        <para>If the polynomial function \(f(x)\) is divided by \(x-c\), then the remainder is \(f(c)\).</para>
      </note>
      <para>Example 2</para>
      <para>Use the Remainder Theorem to find the remainder when \(f(x)=x^3+3x+19\) is divided by \(x + 2\).</para>
      <para>To use the Remainder Theorem, we must use the divisor in the \(x-c\) form. We can write the divisor \(x+2\) as \(x-(-2)\). So, our \(c\) is \(-2\).</para>
      <para>To find the remainder, we evaluate \(f(c)\), which is \(f(-2)\).</para>
      <para>\(f(x)=x^3+3x+19\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> To evaluate \(f(-2)\), substitute \(x=-2\).</para>
      <para> \(f({\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}2})=({\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}2})^3+3({\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}2})+19\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Simplify. </para>
      <para> \(f(-2)=(-8)-6+19\) 
  \(f(-2)=5\)
  The remainder is 5 when \(f(x)=x^3+3x+19\) is divided by \(x+2\).</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Check using synthetic division.</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt=".">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_06_11_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
      </para>
      <newline/>
      <title>Try It: Using the Remainder Theorem</title>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Use the Remainder Theorem to find the remainder when \(f(x)=x^3+4x+15\) is divided by \(x + 2\).</para>
            <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Here is how to solve a problem using the Remainder Theorem:</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=x^3+4x+15\)</para>
            <para>\(f(2)=(-2)^3+4(-2)+15\)</para>
            <para>\(f(2)=-8-8+15= -1\)</para>
            <para>The remainder is \(-1\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
    </section>
  </content>
